# What a fuzzing build uploads to the daemon, which is a different set from
# what the shipped image's build uploads.
#
# BuildKit reads this file instead of the one in the context root whenever a
# build names the Dockerfile beside it. It REPLACES that file rather than
# adding to it: with this file present, not one line of `.dockerignore` at
# the context root applies. So every exclusion that file carries is restated
# below, and the only difference between the two is the two lines named
# under "What the fuzzing build needs back".
#
# That is not a style choice about duplication.
# test/layer-2-integration/fuzz-build-context.test.js derives the set from
# the context root's file, subtracts those two lines, and plants a probe for
# every pattern that remains at the context root and one directory in, then
# renders both contexts with docker. A line dropped from here reappears as a
# planted credential arriving at the daemon.
#
# Pattern syntax is Go's filepath.Match against the whole relative path, so
# `*` never crosses a separator and a bare name reaches the context root
# alone. Every exclusion therefore carries the `**/` prefix, which covers
# the context root and every directory under it.
#
# The last line that matches a path decides it, which is what the re-include
# at the end depends on.

# What the fuzzing build needs back.
#
# The context root's file excludes `**/fuzz` and `**/.clusterfuzzlite`,
# because nothing in the shipped image reads either one and a seed corpus
# grows without bound during a local run. This build is the one that does
# read them: `.clusterfuzzlite/Dockerfile` copies the tree and then
# `.clusterfuzzlite/build.sh`, and that script builds every `fuzz/*.fuzz.js`
# harness with the seed corpus and dictionary beside it. Neither line
# appears below, which is the whole difference.

# Version control and CI definitions.
**/.git
**/.github
**/.gitignore
**/.gitattributes

# Editor, OS and assistant scratch.
**/.vscode
**/.idea
**/.claude
**/.playwright-mcp
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/desktop.ini

# Maintainer-local notes and internal specs. Nothing in the build reads
# them and they are not written for anyone running this server.
**/CLAUDE.md
**/AGENTS.md
**/memory

# Dependency and build output. `build.sh` runs `npm ci` inside the image
# from `fuzz/package-lock.json`, so a developer's local install under
# `fuzz/node_modules` is the jazzer toolchain sent through the context for
# nothing.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/build
**/coverage
**/public/dist

# Registry configuration. The build resolves the harness toolchain from the
# public registry, and this file is where a registry credential lives when
# one exists.
**/.npmrc

# Runtime state. A data directory holds live mail, account credentials and
# sealed DKIM private keys, whether it sits at the container path or at a
# developer's local override.
**/data
**/data-e2e
**/blobs
**/queue
**/tlsrpt
**/tls
**/acme
**/var
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite-wal
**/*.sqlite-shm

# Test fixtures, and the diagnostics the gates persist. A gate log carries
# captured source and whole failure transcripts. The harnesses require
# `lib/`, never `test/`.
**/test
**/.test-output
**/*.log
**/*.pid
**/*.pid.lock

# The reproducer a fuzz run writes when it finds something. libFuzzer puts
# it in the directory the run started from, and for the documented local
# command that is the context root.
**/crash-*
**/leak-*
**/timeout-*
**/oom-*
**/slow-unit-*

# Credential and secret material, excluded at every depth rather than in
# the context root alone.
#
# .env is named because the compose stack reads the vault passphrase from
# the environment and documents a local build from this same directory in
# the same file. An operator who follows both halves has the passphrase
# that seals every key on their data volume sitting in the context root.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/*.cert
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/secrets.json
**/credentials.json
**/id_rsa*
**/id_ed25519*

# Backup and patch artifacts.
**/*.bak
**/*.orig
**/*.rej

# The vendored framework is what every harness requires, and one rule above
# reaches into it: it carries a trust anchor whose extension is on the
# credential list. This must stay last, because the last matching line wins.
#
# It names the certificate shape rather than the subtree, and the difference
# is the whole point of the line. A re-include written as `lib/vendor/**`
# would not restore one anchor: it would cancel every exclusion above it for
# everything under that prefix, and the environment file, private key,
# registry configuration and database withheld everywhere else would be
# uploaded from there.
!lib/vendor/**/*.pem
